\title{Standardize the 990-PF recipient foundation-status field (SIGOCPYRFSTA)}
\description{
Form 990-PF Part XIV Table 3a, column (c) asks the filer for the "foundation
status of recipient". The IRS supplies no controlled vocabulary, so the field
is free text: across 155,000 sample grants it carries ~1,400 distinct
spellings of what are really a handful of concepts. This module collapses
that free text onto a five-level standard, which is what decides whether a
grant is even eligible to appear in the IRS Business Master File.
}
\section{Why a rule cascade and not a lookup table}{

A lookup can only cover the spellings already seen. The rules below key off
the \emph{concept} — an IRC section reference, the words "public charity", the
words "operating foundation" — so unseen spellings land in the same bucket.
Two normalizations do most of the work:
\itemize{
\item \strong{section collapsing} — \code{501(c)(3)}, \verb{501C 3}, \verb{501 C 3}, and
\verb{501c3} all become the single token \verb{501C3}; likewise \verb{509A1}, \verb{170B1A},
\verb{4942J3}. Without this, each spacing variant needs its own pattern.
\item \strong{de-spacing} — a second, space-free copy of the string is matched
in parallel, so \verb{PUB CHAR}, \verb{PUBLIC CH}, and \code{OTHERPUBLICCHARITY} are all
caught by one \code{PUBCHAR} / \code{PUBLICCH} pattern.
}
}

\section{Standard levels (\code{recipient_type})}{

\itemize{
\item \code{public_charity} — nonprofit, public charity (PC, 501(c)(3) with no
foundation qualifier, 509(a)(1)-(3), church, school, hospital)
\item \code{nonprofit_other} — nonprofit, other subsection or exempt-but-
unspecified
\item \code{foundation_pf} — private foundation, private operating foundation,
exempt operating foundation
\item \code{individual} — a natural person
\item \code{other_unknown} — blank, N/A, government, foreign, and text that
answers a different question ("PAID", "CASH", "VARIOUS", "ACTIVE")
}
